 ## Architecture
 
-Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed regardless of the lookup backend. The certificates node uses only this interface.
+Expose `findBySerial(serial)` returning either `Found(record, snapshotDate)` or `NotFound(snapshotDate)`. Record fields and result types are fixed. The certificates node uses only this interface and sees nothing of the storage or query path behind it.
 
-Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend is not selected yet. Candidates are a local embedded SQLite index, an on-site HTTPS directory, and a managed search service. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+Snapshot validation, index preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. Lookup is served from a kiosk-local SQLite index built on the kiosk from the snapshot's records. The index file lives on the kiosk's local disk and is read-only during lookup.
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
-Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
+Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Then build a fresh SQLite index from the snapshot's records in a temporary location, leaving the active index untouched. Activate the new snapshot only after both validation and the index build succeed, by an atomic swap that replaces the active index and its recorded snapshot date together.
+
+Any failure — bad signature, unsupported schema, duplicate serials, or a failed or incomplete index build — leaves the previous index active and reports a useful error. Because the new index is built aside and swapped in whole, a build interrupted partway (including by power loss) never becomes the active index; the previous snapshot remains the one served.
 
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Query the active SQLite index by normalized serial for a single record. Return the active snapshot date in every result, whether or not a record matched.
+
+## Performance
+
+The index holds one row per record keyed by normalized serial, so each lookup is a single indexed point query — which meets the 250 ms lookup goal for 90,000 records within 4 GB of RAM. The cost of reading and indexing the snapshot is paid once per import, not per lookup.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Kiosk-local embedded SQLite index, built from each snapshot | On-site HTTPS directory; managed search service | Both alternatives require runtime network access during lookup, which the no-network tenet forbids, and each adds a service, credentials, or recurring cost to operate. A local index keeps lookup entirely on-disk with nothing to run; the accepted cost is that every kiosk validates and rebuilds its own index on import. |
